<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-2-1-program-design-development">
  <time minutes="45" />
  <title>Program Design and Development</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>In this unit, we will learn how to design and develop programs that meet user needs with  
      more complex Python control structures for selection and repetition 
      In this first lesson, we will focus on the development process, user research, documentation, 
      and reusing and citing digital content. 
    </p>
        <image source="_static/Unit1images/iterative-design.png" alt="Iterative Process" width="50%" />


  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="development-process">
    <title>The Development Process</title>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.A.1</title>
      <p>
        Programmers use a development process to plan and build apps and programs that work well for 
        the people who will use them. A typical process includes understanding the problem and the 
        users, designing a solution, building a first version or prototype, testing it, and then 
        reflecting on what worked and what needs improvement. These phases may occur in different 
        orders and may be repeated.
      </p>
    </note>
  

    <note>
      <title>2.1.A.2</title>
      <p>
        An <term>iterative development process</term> involves revising a program based on feedback, testing, or 
        reflection throughout development. This often requires returning to earlier phases to 
        improve the design or behavior.
      </p>
    </note>

     <p>
      We will use an <term>iterative development process</term> called Design Thinking in the next section 
      where design, implementation, and testing are repeated until the design meets user needs. 
      <term>Iterative</term> means that the process is repeated, and each repetition is called an <term>iteration</term>.
    </p>

     <image source="_static/Unit2images/DesignThinkingimage.png" alt="Design Thinking Process" width="40%" />

    <note>
      <title>2.1.A.3</title>
      <p>
        An <term>incremental development process</term> breaks a problem into smaller features and builds a 
        program in steps. Each part is checked to ensure it works before it is combined into the 
        whole.
      </p>
    </note>

   <p>App development can be iterative and <term>incremental</term> where incremental means that you work on small, manageable parts 
    of the app at a time. For example, you could design the user interface first, then add functionality in subsequent iterations. </p>

 

    <note>
      <title>2.1.A.5</title>
      <p>
        When developing programs with others, teams can use a defined workflow by assigning roles, 
        sharing work, and keeping track of versions so changes can be reviewed and combined 
        responsibly.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>Many software development teams use <term>version control systems</term> like <term>GitHub</term> which is a platform for 
      managing code and collaborating on projects. Github allows programmers to upload their code online, track changes, manage different versions of code, and 
      collaborate with others on the same project. 
       </p>
       
       <image source="_static/Unit2images/github.webp" alt="GitHub diagram" width="50%" />

   
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="design-thinking">
  <title>Design Thinking</title>

  <p><term>Design thinking</term> is a human-centered approach to innovation that involves understanding the user, and iteratively
    designing creative solutions to meet their needs. Some of the world’s leading brands, such as Apple, Google and Samsung, use the 
    design thinking approach. Watch the introduction to Design Thinking <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a7sEoEvT8l8">video</url> below.
  </p>
   <video youtube="a7sEoEvT8l8" label="video-design-thinking" />
   
    <note>
      <title>2.1.B.1</title>
      <p>
        Programmers often begin by identifying intended users and their goals rather than starting 
        with a list of features. Focusing on <term>users</term> and <term>goals</term> helps teams prioritize features and 
        design an app or program that meets real needs.
      </p>
    </note>

   <p>The Design Thinking process has 5 stages: Empathize, Define, Ideate, Prototype, and Test, which are repeated incrementally and
    iteratively until the user needs are met. The following diagram shows the Design Thinking process.
   </p>
     
      <image source="_static/Unit2images/Design-Thinking-Stages.png" alt="Design Thinking Stages" width="70%" />

   <p>There are 5 stages in the Design Thinking process, and it all starts with emphathizing with your users.</p>
   <ol>
			<li><strong>Empathize: </strong>Investigate and understand your users to identify the program requirements</li>
			<ul><li>collect data through surveys</li>
			<li>conduct user testing</li>
			<li>conduct interviews </li>
			<li>make direct observations</li></ul>
			<li><strong>Define:</strong> Analyze the problem and determine the program specifications</li>
			<li><strong>Ideate:</strong> Brainstorm possible solutions</li>
			<ul><li>make a list of your ideas</li>
				<li>draw the interface on paper or online</li>
      </ul>
			<li><strong>Prototype:</strong> Creatively build simple solutions</li>
			<li><strong>Test:</strong> Evaluate the solutions</li>
		</ol>

    
      <project label="design-thinking-chair-project" xml:id="design-chair-project">
        <title>Design Thinking: Chair Project</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In this project, you will use the design thinking process to design a chair for one of the Simpsons characters.
            You will work in groups and choose or be assigned a character and follow the <url 
            href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/16Y9RPf6IhZ4pZRjtosXdergQPCLRgrmY_TTMr4N9xpU/">Design a Simpsons Chair Instructions</url>. 
            After completing the project, reflect on the following questions:
            <ul>
              <li>Describe the characteristics of your user and how your design addresses their needs.</li>
              <li>Did you use iterative design where you changed some aspects of your design during implementation? What did you change?</li>
              <li>Did you use incremental design where you built the chair in steps? What parts did you build in each step? 
                </li>
              <li>How could Gen AI be used in the design process? If you have access to a Gen AI tool, try using it to generate
                ideas for your chair design. How did it help you? What were the limitations?</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <response/>
      </project>
          
</subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="user-research">
    <title>User Research and Feedback</title>


    <note>
      <title>2.1.B.2</title>
      <p>
        <term>User research</term> means learning from the people who will use an app or program. Programmers may 
        gather information from intended users to understand what they need, what limits or 
        challenges they have, and what matters most.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.B.3</title>
      <p>
        <term>Program requirements</term> describe what a program must do and how it should behave. Requirements 
        may be based on user research and may include user interactions a program must support, 
        information a user interface should display, and feedback the app or program should provide.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.B.4</title>
      <p>
        Programmers can use <term>user stories</term> to connect requirements to real tasks and to guide 
        interface design and testing. <term>User stories</term>are hypothetical descriptions of who a user is, 
        what they want to do, and why it is important. Teams may also represent user needs with <term>use 
        cases</term> or <term>storyboards</term>, which show how someone would use the app or program step by step.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>The Simpsons chair design project above presented <term>user stories</term> for each Simpsons character that
    described their needs. <term>Use cases</term> are detailed descriptions of how a user interacts with a system to achieve a 
    specific goal. For example, a non-standard use-case of a chair for a child might be to tip it over and pretend it is a rocket ship. 
    Users sometimes use products in ways that the designers did not anticipate! 
    <term>Storyboards</term> are visual representations of a sequence of events, often used to illustrate how a user interacts with a product or system. 
  </p>

   <image source="_static/Unit2images/user-story-lisa.png" alt="User story for Lisa Simpson" width="90%" />
   
   <note>
      <title>2.1.B.5</title>
      <p>
        <term>User research</term> can be performed in multiple ways, such as surveys, interviews, direct 
        observation, and user testing.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.B.6</title>
      <p>
        The design phase may include activities such as brainstorming and storyboarding, decomposing 
        a problem into subproblems, creating diagrams or sketches of a user interface layout, and 
        developing a testing strategy based on program requirements and user needs.
      </p>
    </note>
  
   <note>
      <title>2.1.A.6</title>
      <p>
        Programmers can gather evidence from users during development by observing where users get 
        stuck or confused. This evidence can guide revisions that improve the usability of apps or 
        programs.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.A.7</title>
      <p>
        Many apps and programs go through revisions and updates to stay useful for users. 
        <term>Maintaining</term> a program involves making changes after a working version exists, such as fixing 
        bugs, improving usability, or adapting the program as user needs or conditions change.
      </p>
    </note>
  
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="documentation">
    <title>Documentation</title>

      <note>
      <title>2.1.A.4</title>
      <p>
        Development processes often include creating and updating design documentation to help track 
        decisions, guide what to build next, and support testing, debugging, maintenance, and 
        improvement.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.C.1</title>
      <p>
        <term>Program documentation</term> is a written description of the purpose and behavior of a code segment 
        or program, including how it is intended to be used, how it accomplishes its task, and how 
        it was developed.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.C.2</title>
      <p>
        <term>Comments</term> are a form of documentation included in code for humans to read. Comments do not 
        affect how a program runs.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.C.3</title>
      <p>
        Not all programming environments support comments within the code. Documentation may also be 
        written in other formats, such as design notes, a test plan, or a change log.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.C.4</title>
      <p>
        Documentation helps programmers understand code that has already been written. Clear 
        descriptions of purpose and behavior make it easier for a programmer to understand and 
        update code without having to figure out how it works.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.C.5</title>
      <p>
        Useful documentation may describe important details such as a program's overall purpose, key 
        variables and algorithms, expected inputs, and expected outputs. Comments can clarify the 
        purpose or behavior of specific abstractions or sections of code. Clear and concise 
        documentation helps others understand, use, and update a program correctly.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.C.6</title>
      <p>
        Programmers should document a program throughout development to improve clarity, support 
        collaboration, and record decisions and resources used, such as code written by others and 
        AI tool use.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Reusing and Citing Digital Content</title>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.D.1</title>
      <p>
        Programmers often reuse or build on existing code, images, sounds, and other digital content 
        because it can save time, improve reliability, and help them meet user needs without 
        reinventing common solutions. Because programmers often build with existing online tools and 
        code, they should acknowledge code that is reused, adapted, or co-developed, including code 
        from other people, online sources, or AI tools.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.D.2</title>
      <p>
        Digital works are considered the intellectual property of their copyright owner, who is 
        often the work's creator but may be another person or organization. This means the copyright 
        owner has legal control over the original work and can decide whether others may use, share, 
        modify, or distribute it. To respect intellectual property, people should follow a work's 
        license or obtain permission before reusing material, and provide attribution when required.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.D.3</title>
      <p>
        Digital content can be copied, edited, and shared quickly, which can make it harder to 
        determine who owns it and what permissions are required. Content is also often shared online 
        without crediting the creator or getting permission.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.D.4</title>
      <p>
        Despite how easy it is to share content online, presenting material created by someone else 
        as your own is plagiarism. Using another person's work without permission or proper credit 
        may have legal consequences.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.D.5</title>
      <p>
        Knowing how to reuse and cite digital content correctly helps programmers build projects 
        more efficiently while avoiding plagiarism and misuse. Following reuse and attribution 
        requirements helps others understand what was reused, where it came from, and what 
        permissions apply, which supports transparency and responsible sharing.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.D.6</title>
      <p>
        Some licenses and tools make reuse easier by clearly stating permissions. Open source and 
        Creative Commons licenses provide standardized terms that tell others how they may use, 
        modify, and redistribute software or creative works.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.D.7</title>
      <p>
        License terms vary in what they allow and require. Some licenses require attribution, while 
        others allow unrestricted reuse. Creative Commons Zero (CC0) allows creators to waive 
        copyright restrictions to the extent possible so others can reuse the work.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>2.1.D.8</title>
      <p>
        To decide whether reuse or sharing is appropriate, people should identify the source and its 
        license or permissions, follow any requirements, and avoid using material in ways the 
        license does not allow.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

   <subsection xml:id="LOs-2-1">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>
    <remark>
      <title>2.1.A</title>
      <p>
        Develop a program using an iterative or incremental process.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>2.1.B</title>
      <p>
        Design an app or program and its user interface based on user research and program requirements.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>2.1.C</title>
      <p>
        Describe the purpose and behavior of a code segment or program by writing documentation.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>2.1.D</title>
      <p>
        Determine when it is appropriate to reuse, cite, and share digital content.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>
</section>
